        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">COPD exacerbation, treated with bronchodilators and steroids</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear Colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospitalisation of your 84-year-old male patient, admitted on 28th April 2019 with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Clinical examination, chest X-ray, arterial blood gas analysis, and pulse oximetry confirmed an acute exacerbation without pneumonic infiltrate. Spirometry supported severely impaired airflow obstruction. He was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, supplemental oxygen therapy, and intravenous fluids. Blood tests were obtained to exclude infectious or metabolic co-morbidities. The patient responded well to treatment with significant symptomatic improvement. He was discharged on 29th April 2019 with optimised inhaler therapy and a short oral steroid course. Patient education regarding inhaler technique and exacerbation self-management was provided. Please arrange early follow-up and consider pulmonary rehabilitation referral.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
